To set up Laravel Dusk on an AWS EC2 server, follow these steps:

1. Establish an SSH connection to your EC2 instance using a tool like PuTTY (for Windows) or Terminal (for macOS/Linux); or use the AWS Management Console for SSH connection.
2. Install PHP version 7.4 or higher, and Composer on your server by following these steps:

   - On Ubuntu:

     ```
     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install software-properties-common
     sudo add-apt-repository ppa:ondrej/php
     sudo apt install php7.4
     sudo apt install curl
     curl -sS https://getcomposer.org/installer | sudo php -- --install-dir=/usr/local/bin --filename=composer
     ```

   - On CentOS:

     ```
     sudo yum install epel-release
     sudo yum install dnf-utils
     sudo dnf module reset php
     sudo dnf module enable php:7.4
     sudo dnf install php php-pdo php-json
     sudo yum install curl
     sudo curl -sS https://getcomposer.org/installer | sudo php -- --install-dir=/usr/bin --filename=composer
     ```

3. Using Composer, install Laravel and Laravel Dusk by running the following command, substituting `your-project-name` with the desired project name:

   ```
   sudo composer create-project --prefer-dist laravel/laravel your-project-name
   cd your-project-name
   composer require --dev laravel/dusk
   ```

4. Generate an application encryption key using Laravel's `key:generate` Artisan command:

   ```
   php artisan key:generate
   ```

   This command generates a `:/path/to/your-project-name/.env` file, which stores your application key.

5. Configure your application `.env` file to include Dusk-related settings:

   Edit the `.env` file and add/update the following options:

   ```
   # Dusk Configuration
   DUSK_DRIVER=tightenco/jasny-bootstrap
   APP_URL=http://your-domain.com
   DUSK_HEADLESS=true
   ```

   Replace `your-domain.com` with the actual domain or IP address of your EC2 instance.

   Optionally, add the relevant database settings for your Laravel application.

6. Install and configure a web server (Apache or Nginx) and PHP-FPM on your EC2 instance, so that your Laravel application can be accessed via a web browser.

   Refer to these AWS documentation on how to install and configure these services:

   - Apache: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/install-LAMP.html#install_apache.ec2user
   - Nginx: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/nginx.html

7. Ensure that your firewall settings allow incoming traffic to your EC2 instance's web server port (80 for HTTP, 443 for HTTPS).

